//# #2475 (Codex on #2819): `Option` is NOT in `resolve_builtin` -- it is
//# hard-coded in the APPLIED door of `resolve_type_expr_core_shadowed`, at
//# arity 1 only. So `enum Option[T]` collides even though the name-only
//# registry does not know the spelling at all.
//#
//# The `enum` spelling is here because it carries the decisive diagnostic:
//# without the refusal this program is rejected by `match` instead, with
//# "variant `Some` of Option is not handled" -- the builtin's shape, reported
//# against a declaration the source really does write. The `struct` spelling
//# collides the same way and is covered by
//# err_type_builtin_spelling_applied_array's sibling reasoning.
//#
//# `StringMap`, the third head hard-coded in that same door, does NOT collide:
//# its arm consults `find_typedef` first, which #2263 decided on purpose and
//# string_map_local_shadow_ok pins as `ok`.
//#
//# Arity is what makes this precise in the other direction: `struct Array[K,
//# V]` resolves through `find_typedef` and stays legal, as does a bare
//# `struct Option` and an applied `Int[T]` -- pinned as `ok` by
//# builtin_spelling_arity2_ok, builtin_spelling_bare_option_ok and
//# builtin_spelling_applied_int_ok, each of which NAMES its type in an
//# annotation rather than only inferring it.
enum Option[T] {
  Yes(T);
  Nope
}

export fn main() -> Int {
  0
}
